<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Namdev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1270–<a href="/years/1350">1350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Namdeo, Namadeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Namdev was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> poet-saint of the Varkari tradition and a devotee of the god Vithoba of Pandharpur; tradition dates his life to 1270–1350, but scholars have proposed dates from the 13th to the 15th century. Born into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> of tailors, he composed simply worded songs of praise and taught that all could be saved by devotion, without regard to caste. His companions in worship included Gora, a potter, Janabai, a maid, and Chokhamela, of a caste treated as untouchable. Sixty-one songs under his name are in the Adi Granth, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/namdev.jpg" alt="Namdev" width="320" height="552" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Bhagat_Namdev_from_a_fresco_depicting_Guru_Arjan_meeting_the_Bhagats_and_composing_the_Adi_Granth_with_the_assistance_of_Bhai_Gurdas,_circa_mid-19th_century.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
